==== NAME
mount.pdxfs — attach a `KIND_VOLUME` capability to a VFS mount point,
with audit-first `PdxFsMountRecord@0.1` journaling and elevate-gated
writes to protected trees.

==== SYNOPSIS
    mount.pdxfs [--ro] [--noexec] [--verbose] [--dry-run] <volume-cap> <mount-point>

`<volume-cap>` is a `KIND_VOLUME` cap URI (`cap:volume:0xNN`, hex slot
number) looked up in the invoker's cap environment. `<mount-point>` is
the VFS path the volume should attach at.

==== DESCRIPTION
`mount.pdxfs` is the second of three actions that bring a blank disk
(or image file) online for PdxFS v1: format (`mkfs.pdxfs`), then
**mount** (this tool), with `umount.pdxfs` as the reversal. It is a
standalone ring-3 ELF — not a library — that issues its own syscalls
directly, matching every other `src/user/*.pdx` tool in the paideia-os
monorepo.

Every invocation follows the same six-phase pipeline: parse argv, open
one shared `libpdx-audit` INTENT record, classify `<mount-point>`'s
§4.2 mount-point class, resolve + narrow `<volume-cap>` via
`libpdx-volume`, attempt `sys_mount` (or skip it under `--dry-run`), and
emit exactly one `PdxFsMountRecord@0.1` line plus the matching audit
RESULT record before exiting. See [[cref: MOUNT-POINT-CLASSES]] for the
four-class table, [[cref: RESULT-CODES]] for the full failure taxonomy,
and [[cref: LIMITATIONS]] for what is stubbed or deferred at v1.0.0.

==== OPTIONS
---- --ro
Mount read-only. Sets bit 0 of the internal flags bitmask.

---- --noexec
Disallow execution of files on the mounted volume. Sets bit 1.

---- --verbose
Reserved for future diagnostic output. Sets bit 2; no behavior change
at v1.0.0.

---- --dry-run
Skip the real `sys_mount` syscall entirely. Every other pipeline phase
(argv parse, audit INTENT/RESULT, mount-point classification,
volume-cap resolve) still runs for real; only the syscall itself is
skipped. Sets bit 3. `result_code: DRY_RUN` is printed unconditionally
on this path, regardless of what a real attempt would have produced.

==== MOUNT-POINT-CLASSES
Four buckets (§4.2 of `design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md`, paideia-os
— this tool's own scope is narrower than that design doc's full
five-class table; see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]]):

    MPC_USER_SUBTREE (0)  -- /home/**, /mnt/**, /tmp/**. No elevate.
    MPC_SYSTEM_PATH  (1)  -- /system/**, /boot/**, /dev/**. Elevate
                             required -- see [[cref: ELEVATE]].
    MPC_CROSS_USER   (2)  -- another user's /home/** subtree. Defined
                             but NEVER PRODUCED at v1.0.0 -- no
                             KIND_USER identity lookup exists to tell
                             an own-subtree path apart from another
                             user's; every /home/** path classifies as
                             MPC_USER_SUBTREE today.
    MPC_INVALID      (3)  -- anything matching none of the six known
                             prefixes above. result_code: INVALID_
                             MOUNT_POINT, exit 3.

==== ELEVATE
`MPC_SYSTEM_PATH` (and, once reachable, `MPC_CROSS_USER`) trigger a
call into `libpdx-elevate`'s `elevate_client_acquire` flow -- but this
tool holds no `KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL` broker-endpoint capability
(`caps.decl`'s own note: still a PLACEHOLDER at v1.0.0), so the call
site is a documented FAIL-CLOSED STUB: it always denies. Every elevate-
required mount point therefore produces `result_code: ELEVATION_STUB`
and a non-zero exit (4) -- NOT `ELEVATION_REQUIRED` (an older, distinct
code from a prior milestone meaning "this tool refuses to even try");
`ELEVATION_STUB` means "this tool classified the mount point correctly
and knows elevation is genuinely needed, but the broker hop itself is
unprovisioned." See [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].

==== RESULT-CODES
Every invocation prints exactly one
`PdxFsMountRecord@0.1 { volume: ..., mount_point: ..., flags: ...,
result_code: <CODE> [, mount_id: <id>] }` line (preceded by a
semantic-pipe deferral header -- see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]]). The full
`result_code` vocabulary, by numeric value:

    0  DRY_RUN               -- --dry-run given; no syscall issued.
    1  OK                    -- real success. mount_id is printed.
                                 Unreachable at v1.0.0 (dispatch_mount
                                 is kernel-side unwired).
    2  ELEVATION_REQUIRED    -- reserved; no code path in this repo
                                 emits this any more (superseded by
                                 ELEVATION_STUB below).
    3  KERNEL_ERROR          -- generic fallback for any kernel errno
                                 not covered by a more specific code.
    4  ELEVATION_STUB        -- mount point needs elevate; the
                                 fail-closed libpdx-elevate stub denied.
    5  INVALID_MOUNT_POINT   -- mount point matched none of the six
                                 known §4.2 prefixes.
    6  BAD_VOLUME_CAP        -- <volume-cap> failed to parse as
                                 `cap:volume:0xNN`.
    7  NARROW_FAILED         -- libpdx-volume.vol_kind_narrow refused
                                 the requested ops_mask.
    8  EINVAL_BACKEND        -- sys_mount: SYS_MOUNT_EINVAL_BACKEND.
    9  EINVAL_MP_LEN         -- sys_mount: SYS_MOUNT_EINVAL_MP_LEN.
    10 EINVAL_DEV_LEN        -- sys_mount: SYS_MOUNT_EINVAL_DEV_LEN.
    11 UNIMPL_BACKEND        -- sys_mount: SYS_MOUNT_UNIMPL_PDXFS_BLOCK.
    12 MOUNT_FAIL            -- sys_mount: SYS_MOUNT_MOUNT_FAIL.
    13 EFAULT                -- sys_mount: SYS_MOUNT_EFAULT.
    14 ENOSYS                -- raw -ENOSYS. THE ONE REAL, OBSERVED
                                 OUTCOME at v1.0.0 for any well-formed
                                 non-dry-run, non-elevate-gated
                                 invocation -- dispatch_mount is still
                                 an unconditional -ENOSYS stub
                                 kernel-side.
    15 AUDIT_FAIL            -- reserved for a future stricter
                                 audit-gated-output mode; never emitted
                                 by any v1.0.0 call site.

Exit codes: 0 (DRY_RUN/OK), 2 (missing positional argv), 3 (any
kernel-error-family code: 3, 5, 6, 7, 8..15), 4 (ELEVATION_STUB or the
now-unreachable ELEVATION_REQUIRED).

==== AUDIT
Every invocation opens exactly one `libpdx-audit` record before any
dispatch (`op_name` = `"mount.pdxfs.mount"` or
`"mount.pdxfs.mount.dry_run"`, `op_args` = the real `<mount-point>`
string) and commits it on every terminal path with the real process
exit status -- one shared `audit_id` links the INTENT event (open) and
the RESULT event (commit). The kernel-side audit-journal broker
dispatch is confirmed stubbed as of v1.0.0 (`libpdx-audit`'s own
STATUS.md), so every audit call may no-op at the daemon; this tool
treats that as an expected, non-fatal outcome (a zero `audit_id` from
`audit_begin` is threaded through unexamined, and `audit_commit` is
simply skipped for it).

==== LIMITATIONS
Cited from `design/architecture.md` / `STATUS.md`; each item names the
milestone with the full rationale.

- **No real end-to-end mount.** `sys_mount` (sysno 75) is
  dispatch-unwired kernel-side (`dispatch_mount` is an unconditional
  `-ENOSYS` stub); even once wired, its real ABI does not accept a
  `KIND_VOLUME` cap slot, contradicting the cap-slot contract this
  tool's own call shape targets (M2-002, M3-004).
- **`KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE` row append is a best-effort no-op.**
  `PMT_OP_APPEND_ROW` is reserved but refused kernel-side; this tool's
  own call site discards the result (M2-002).
- **`libpdx-elevate` is never actually dispatched.** No
  `KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL` broker-endpoint cap is provisioned for this
  tool; `mount_elev_require_system` always denies (M3-001).
- **No cross-user `/home/**` distinction.** `MPC_CROSS_USER` is a
  defined but unreachable classification; no `KIND_USER` identity
  lookup exists in this tool (M2-003, unchanged at M3-001).
- **No `libpdx-semantic-pipe` binding.** `Registry::bind_by_name` is
  confirmed inert (`paideia-os#2000`); every record is the line-based
  fallback rendering, preceded by a documented deferral header
  (M3-003).
- **No independent `KIND_VOLUME` kind check.** No `cap_check_kind`-
  style kernel primitive exists anywhere in the tree; a cap slot of the
  wrong kind narrows "successfully" today and only fails once (a real)
  `sys_mount` itself would reject it (M2-001).
- **The dual-signed release manifest is not actually signed at
  v1.0.0.** `release/manifest.pdxsig.txt` ships with every hash and
  signature slot as a documented placeholder (M5-001).

==== SEE-ALSO
Upstream design: `design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md` §4 + §9.3 and
`design/tooling/volume-lifecycle-mechanism.md` in the paideia-os repo.
Repo-internal shape: `design/architecture.md` in this repo. Per-driver
test contracts: `tests/README.md`. Release workflow:
`release/RELEASE-1.0.0.md`. Sibling tools: `mkfs.pdxfs` (format),
`umount.pdxfs` (reversal, not yet a repo). Shared library:
`libpdx-volume`.

==== VERSION
mount.pdxfs 1.0.0 (R53 wave, M5-001/M5-002 signed-release scaffold). The
actual dual-sign + mirror-push run happens once substrates S1
(paideia-as toolchain), S2 (pkgs.paideia-os endpoint), and S5
(a real `KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL` broker cap for this tool) go green, per
`release/RELEASE-1.0.0.md` §3.
